      "content": "# Heating and Air Conditioning Service in Queens, NY\nCentral air, furnaces, boilers and ductless systems for houses, two-family homes and apartment buildings across Queens.\nService area\n## Your Local HVAC Contractor in Queens\nQueens has more single- and two-family houses than any other borough, which means more furnaces, central air systems and attic or basement ductwork — alongside the high-rises of Long Island City and the apartment buildings of Jackson Heights and Rego Park.\nWhether you need a repair on a 20-year-old furnace or are weighing a full replacement, we start by checking the whole system — equipment, ductwork and controls — so you only pay for what actually fixes the problem.\n### Neighborhoods we serve in Queens\n- Astoria\n- Long Island City\n- Sunnyside\n- Woodside\n- Jackson Heights\n- Forest Hills\n- Rego Park\n- Flushing\n- Bayside\n- Ridgewood\n- Jamaica\n- Kew Gardens\n## Homes and Buildings We Work On in Queens\n### Detached and semi-detached houses\nForced-air furnaces with central air are typical in Bayside, Forest Hills and Flushing. We repair, tune up and replace both, and size new equipment to the house rather than the old unit.\n### Two-family homes\nSeparate systems or zoning let each unit control its own temperature and pay its own bills. Ductless systems are a clean option where ducts can’t be added.\n### Boiler-heated homes\nMany Astoria, Sunnyside and Ridgewood homes heat with hot-water or steam boilers. We service them and add ductless cooling without disturbing the radiators.\n### High-rise apartments and condos\nLong Island City buildings often use fan coils, PTACs or VRF. We work within building rules on access and scheduling.\n## Questions from Queens Customers\n### Should I replace my furnace and air conditioner at the same time?\nIf both are near the end of their life, replacing them together usually costs less overall and gives you a matched, more efficient system. If one is newer, it can often stay. We’ll show you the condition of each before you decide.\n### Can each apartment in a two-family house have its own controls?\nYes. Either separate systems or a zoned system with its own thermostat per floor lets each household set its own temperature. Ductless mini-splits make this straightforward where there is no ductwork.\n## Other Areas We Serve\n- HVAC in Brooklyn"
